  "query_preview": "..\n\nThrough next Monday...\n\nA change in the weather pattern begins tonight...finally.\n\nWarm & dry weather continues for much of today, with high temps \nmostly 50s. Satellite imagery shows an advancing trof along the \nPacific coast and moist southwest flow from CA to MT. Pwats \nexpected to rise to near 200% of normal over the next 24 hours. A \nperiod of large scale ascent will lift across the area bringing \nrain/snow tonight through Wednesday. In fact, we should start \nseeing precip eke into the far west by sunset. Mountain snowfall \nshould be in the 3-6\" range, but higher amounts (50% chance) could\noccur in W-NW favored aspects of the Absarokas south of \nLivingston. A southerly oriented upper level jet lifting thru \ncentral MT should slow the eastward progression of lower level \nbaroclinicity, meaning as colder air advects in the evening we \nshould see western foothills change to wet snow before the descent\narrives. Will make no changes to the Winter Weather Advisory \nalready issued for the Paradise Valley, Livingston Area, Northern \nPark and Judith Gap zones. The latter will see gusty north winds \nand some blowing snow as well. Remaining lower elevations will see\nmostly rain but dynamic cooling as a mid level circulation \ndevelops somewhere in the vicinity of far southeast MT Wednesday \nmorning, may allow for wet snow over the higher hills east of \nBillings, down to 4000ft or so. This is still an area of \nuncertainty among models. If the low lifts into southeast MT a bit\nfurther west, even the hills around Billings could see their \nfirst measurable snow of the season. Something to keep an eye on. \nPrecipitation has trended higher per the stronger forcing. \nEnsembles generally show amounts from 0.10\" in the east to near \n0.50\" in the west. Sheridan and far southeast MT may or may not be\ndry-slotted per the track of the low. Post-frontal W-NW winds \nwill increase on Wednesday with widespread 30-50 mph gusts \nexpected. As it looks now, the risk of 50+ mph gusts is highest \n(40% chance) in Wheatland County and in the far east. Do not see \nenough mid level wind to support much higher gusts than lower 50s.\nWednesday high temps in the low-mid 40s combined with the wind \nwill feel quite chilly.\n\nPrecip ends in the east by Wednesday evening. Ridging should bring\na brief period of dry/cool weather Wednesday night into Thursday.\nFog is a possibility early Thursday morning.\n\nSpecifics remain highly uncertain from Thursday night through the\nweekend. Looking at the longwave pattern, as a strong height\nanomaly exists over far NW Canada, a deeper Pacific trof moves\nthru the intermountain west in some fashion, while a weaker but \ncooler trof sags out of western Canada and attempts to phase with\nthe initial trof. To add to the chaos is another Pacific trof \nthat may move inland by Sunday/Monday. None of this is looking\nparticularly dynamic in our region, but temps will confidently be\ncool enough for light snow if there is a period of ascent strong \nenough to produce precip. Upslope winds would add to this \npotential. Ensembles show high spread but overall not a great\nprecip signal. The greatest chance of snow through this entire\nperiod is over the mountains. Generally speaking, look for\nslightly cooler than normal temps and a low chance (10-30%) of \nlight snow Thursday night to Monday, until more details begin to\nemerge.\n\nJKL",
